WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.720
Did you know the Vikings, a group most&nbsp;
known for their pillaging and raiding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.040
carried around little spoons they used to clean&nbsp;
out their ears? Or that the Egyptians used a kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.640
of ancient breath mint to smell better? Too&nbsp;
often, many of us assumed past societies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.400
dirty and germ infested. But truthfully, there's&nbsp;
a lot of ancient hygiene practices which were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:31.280
surprisingly inventive. And some which are maybe&nbsp;
better left in the past. So let's take a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.360
a few of them. Starting off by unpacking those&nbsp;
Viking ear-spoons a bit more, these small metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.120
objects worked a bit like a modern q-tip. These&nbsp;
ear spoons were clearly treasured possessions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.200
with ornate handles containing intricate carvings.&nbsp;
They've been found in a lot of burial sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.320
alongside the stuff you might more expect&nbsp;
Vikings to be buried with: like swords and coins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.720
Despite stereotypes, the vikings were&nbsp;
actually fairly clean in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.560
with records indicating they took pride&nbsp;
in their well-groomed hair and beards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.960
Moving on to ancient Egypt we have records&nbsp;
dating as far back as 1500 BCE describing...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.200
breath mints. They're called Kapets, and were&nbsp;
made with ingredients like cinnamon, dried reed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.480
honey, and frankincense, which were boiled and&nbsp;
then shaped into little candy balls. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:28.160
used much like modern mints, intended to fight&nbsp;
bad breath and sweeten the smell of the mouth. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.840
even have full surviving recipes from the era, so&nbsp;
you can actually make them if you're interested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.360
Here's another surprising one: the&nbsp;
ancient Romans invented what was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.560
essentially, a laundry mat. Called fullonicae,&nbsp;
they were a place Romans would take their dirty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.440
laundry to for professional cleaning.&nbsp;
The laundry process was quite complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.480
with one step of the procedure involving putting&nbsp;
clothes in tubs filled with a mixture of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.160
alkaline elements, and human urine. Workers would&nbsp;
then go barefoot, and essentially stomp on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.840
soap clothes over and over again until the dirt&nbsp;
scrubbed off. This might seem... a little gross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.520
to our modern sensibilities, but it was actually&nbsp;
a pretty clever way to clean garments. Since human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.280
urine contains ammonia -- and reportedly, the&nbsp;
process resulted in the cleanest of fabrics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.040
Further back in ancient Greece we have&nbsp;
a... rather unusual way to clean your body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.320
This device is called a strigil, and although it&nbsp;
might look like a weapon the Greeks used it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.760
scrape dirt and oil off their bodies following&nbsp;
strenuous physical activity. The Romans used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.760
these too, and the strigils were associated with&nbsp;
athletes, especially. The oily sludge they scraped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.280
off was saved in jars, due to myths they possessed&nbsp;
healing properties. Although it might seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.400
a crude way of cleaning oneself, thankfully it&nbsp;
was usually followed by a more traditional bath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.920
Another ancient Greek and roman hygiene&nbsp;
contraption i feel I'm obligated to mention is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.440
xylospongium, also known as a sponge on a stick:&nbsp;
which makes sense because that's exactly what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.120
was. For better or worse, you might have heard of&nbsp;
this one before. The xylospongium was mainly used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:14.000
to wipe oneself after defecating. The xylospongium&nbsp;
was also shared among people in public latrines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.960
as you can imagine this communal sponge was&nbsp;
a breeding ground for bacteria and deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:26.000
disease. This is a hygiene habit, better left&nbsp;
forgotten. Heading to china in 6th century CE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.440
we have documented evidence of the use of toilet&nbsp;
paper: beating the rest of the world by quite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.880
significant margin. Seeing how the production&nbsp;
of paper originated in china, this does make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.720
some sense. Toilet paper didn't become common for&nbsp;
the lower classes for several centuries, however.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.800
Still, toilet paper didn't become common in the&nbsp;
U.S., for example, until the 19th century -- over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.200
a thousand years later. Ending our voyage&nbsp;
through ancient hygiene with the Aztec empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:02.320
we have a record of the ingenious Aztecs using&nbsp;
what is essentially deodorant. 14th century herbal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.080
text described the importance of battling underarm&nbsp;
odor, outlining a mixture made from pulverized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.600
plants and flowers effective against masking the&nbsp;
scent of sweat when applied under the armpits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.320
This is pretty ahead of its time,&nbsp;
and indeed there are some potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.040
related ethnographic accounts of the Aztecs&nbsp;
finding the smell of the incoming Spanish...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:29.200
pretty appalling.. And that's where a discussion&nbsp;
of ancient hygiene practices comes to an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.880
I hope you enjoyed this video and maybe learned&nbsp;
something new. If you did consider subscribing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:41.840
and leaving this video a like. Thank you so much&nbsp;
for watching, and i'll see you in the next video.

